.TH LATTICEWM 1 "2026-07-31" "LatticeWM 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
latticewm \- an extensible window manager for the river Wayland compositor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B river \-c
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B latticewm
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B LatticeWM
is a window manager for
.BR river (1),
written in Common Lisp.  It is started by river rather than run on its own:
river is the compositor, LatticeWM decides where the windows go.
.PP
Its layout model is a replaceable policy layer rather than the program itself.
The shipped layer is conventional \(em recursive splits, tabs, workspaces,
floating, minimise, multi-monitor.  Loading the
.B lattice
system on top turns each workspace into an infinite plane of cells addressed by
integer coordinate, with zoom, pan and a drawn map.
.PP
Every decision it makes is a generic function you can add a method to, and it
carries a live Lisp image, so a method added at a REPL takes effect on the
running desktop without a restart.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-write\-config
Write a commented starter configuration to
.I ~/.config/latticewm/init.lisp
and exit.  Never overwrites an existing one.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Start without reading the configuration file.  Use this when a change to it
has broken something.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-restore
Start with an empty layout instead of the one saved at the last exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-swank\-port " N"
Listen for a SLIME/SWANK connection on port
.IR N ,
4005 by default.  0 disables it.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " LEVEL"
One of debug, info, warn, error or none.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-options
Print every configuration value with its default and its documentation.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-commands
Print every command with its documentation.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-keys
Print every key binding.
.TP
.B \-\-extension\-surface
Print every generic function that can be specialized, generated from the
running image.
.SH KEYS
The complete keymap is drawn on screen by
.BR Super+/ ,
built from the live bindings, so it includes anything you added yourself.
A few worth knowing before that:
.TP
.B Super+Return
A terminal.
.TP
.B Super+x
Run a command by name.  Emacs's M\-x; Tab completes, and a command that takes
arguments asks for them.
.TP
.B Super+;
Evaluate a Lisp expression in the running window manager.
.TP
.B Super+?
Ask about something: b for every binding, k for a key, c for a command,
a to search, o for a configuration value, s to change one.
.TP
.B Super+h j k l
Move the focus left, down, up, right.  The arrow keys do the same.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/latticewm/init.lisp
The configuration file.  Plain Common Lisp, read in the LATTICEWM/USER package.
Errors in it are logged and do not stop startup.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/latticewm/state.lisp
The saved layout, written at exit and restored at startup.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration directory is looked for, if set.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run it nested inside an existing Wayland session, which is a real compositor
and a real protocol and needs no second machine:
.PP
.RS
.B river \-c latticewm
.RE
.PP
Change something on the running desktop, from a shell:
.PP
.RS
.B wmeval '(setf *gaps* 8)' '(relayout :force t)'
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR river (1),
.BR sbcl (1)
.PP
.I doc/EXTENDING.org
in the source tree is the guide to changing it, with worked examples.
.SH AUTHOR
Shaul Khayyat.
